WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.798
Friday afternoon.

2
00:00:03.798 --> 00:00:07.351
Your new script for the shop's nightly job won't start.

3
00:00:07.351 --> 00:00:09.547
The screen says Permission denied.

4
00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:14.179
Down the hall, an old worker program is still running, and won't stop.

5
00:00:14.179 --> 00:00:15.817
Everyone wants to go home.

6
00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:17.917
Two shortcuts are right there.

7
00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:20.241
Borrow the master key for everything.

8
00:00:20.241 --> 00:00:23.947
Or call security, and have the worker dragged out mid-task.

9
00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:27.257
In the terminal, those are sudo and kill -9.

10
00:00:27.257 --> 00:00:30.559
Both work at once, which is why they feel safe.

11
00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:32.791
And both leave a mess for Monday.

12
00:00:32.791 --> 00:00:35.367
Let's take the two problems one at a time.

13
00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:41.574
In our office building, everyone wears a badge, and every drawer belongs to someone.

14
00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:45.759
In Linux, every file and every running program belongs to a user.

15
00:00:45.967 --> 00:00:53.461
When a program opens a file, the kernel, the core of the operating system, checks the badge against the sign on the drawer.

16
00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:58.142
The building manager with the master key is called root, user ID zero.

17
00:00:58.142 --> 00:01:01.019
The kernel skips almost every check for root.

18
00:01:01.833 --> 00:01:04.419
So let's read the sign, with ls -l.

19
00:01:04.419 --> 00:01:06.561
It looks like noise at first.

20
00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:11.573
The first character is the type: a dash for a file, d for a directory.

21
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.414
Then three groups of three.

22
00:01:13.414 --> 00:01:17.957
What the owner may do, what the group may do, and what everyone else may do.

23
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:22.220
R is read, W is write, X is execute, which means run.

24
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.132
A dash means not allowed.

25
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:26.527
Then come the owner and the group.

26
00:01:26.527 --> 00:01:28.592
A group is a named set of users.

27
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:37.862
For each check, the kernel picks exactly one group of three: the owner's if you own the file, else the group's if you're in it, else everyone else's.

28
00:01:37.862 --> 00:01:39.565
The first that fits decides.

29
00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:41.406
Now back to Friday.

30
00:01:41.406 --> 00:01:45.645
You are the owner, and running the script says Permission denied.

31
00:01:45.645 --> 00:01:46.949
Which slot is empty?

32
00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:51.332
The owner's X.

33
00:01:51.332 --> 00:01:53.414
Nobody may run this file.

34
00:01:53.414 --> 00:01:54.496
Not even you.

35
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:55.703
That's normal.

36
00:01:55.703 --> 00:02:05.234
Programs usually ask for six six six, read and write for everyone, and a setting called the umask removes write for group and others.

37
00:02:05.234 --> 00:02:08.459
So a fresh file is 644, with no X for anyone.

38
00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:10.681
Where does 644 come from?

39
00:02:10.681 --> 00:02:13.098
R is worth four, W two, X one.

40
00:02:13.098 --> 00:02:15.193
Add the slots that are on.

41
00:02:15.193 --> 00:02:17.046
Read plus write is six.

42
00:02:17.046 --> 00:02:18.577
Read alone is four.

43
00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:27.020
Three you'll meet all the time: seven five five for scripts and folders, six four four for files, six oh oh for secrets.

44
00:02:27.633 --> 00:02:29.054
Now the shortcuts.

45
00:02:29.054 --> 00:02:34.736
sudo hides the real cause, and leaves behind files only root can change.

46
00:02:34.736 --> 00:02:39.234
chmod 777 lets every user on the machine change the file.

47
00:02:39.234 --> 00:02:41.365
That's not fixing the lock.

48
00:02:41.365 --> 00:02:43.496
That's taking the door off.

49
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.357
The real fix is one missing bit.

50
00:02:46.357 --> 00:02:49.678
chmod, U plus X: give the owner execute.

51
00:02:49.678 --> 00:02:51.421
Nothing else changes.

52
00:02:51.633 --> 00:02:54.866
Fix the one bit that is missing.

53
00:02:54.866 --> 00:02:57.492
Never reach for chmod 777.

54
00:02:58.300 --> 00:02:59.958
Now the second problem.

55
00:02:59.958 --> 00:03:04.210
A program is a job description in a drawer: a file on disk.

56
00:03:04.210 --> 00:03:11.056
A process is one running copy, a worker on shift, with a number called its PID, its process ID.

57
00:03:11.267 --> 00:03:15.027
The lesson's worker.sh is a pretend background worker.

58
00:03:15.027 --> 00:03:19.067
Every two seconds it processes one job, and writes a line.

59
00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:23.757
There are two ways to end a shift.

60
00:03:23.757 --> 00:03:26.523
Knock, and say: please finish up and go home.

61
00:03:26.733 --> 00:03:30.468
Or call security, and have them carried out mid-sentence.

62
00:03:30.468 --> 00:03:31.910
One leaves tidy desks.

63
00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:34.924
The other leaves half-filed papers everywhere.

64
00:03:35.133 --> 00:03:40.024
That knock is a signal: a short message the kernel delivers to a process.

65
00:03:40.024 --> 00:03:42.837
The polite one is SIGTERM, number fifteen.

66
00:03:42.837 --> 00:03:44.177
Plain kill sends it.

67
00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.221
Security is SIGKILL, number nine, sent with kill -9.

68
00:03:48.221 --> 00:03:51.161
It cannot be caught, so no cleanup runs.

69
00:03:51.161 --> 00:03:53.807
And Ctrl+C sends SIGINT, number two.

70
00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:57.956
worker.sh catches SIGTERM with a shell command called trap.

71
00:03:57.956 --> 00:04:01.346
It's on job three, and you knock with a plain kill.

72
00:04:01.346 --> 00:04:03.141
What does the log say next?

73
00:04:06.367 --> 00:04:09.473
It finishes job three, says so, and leaves.

74
00:04:09.473 --> 00:04:11.785
Stopped cleanly after job three.

75
00:04:11.785 --> 00:04:14.024
Exit code zero, a clean ending.

76
00:04:14.633 --> 00:04:18.535
Run it again, and on job three, send kill -9.

77
00:04:18.535 --> 00:04:20.963
The log just stops, mid-job.

78
00:04:20.963 --> 00:04:23.131
Nobody finished anything.

79
00:04:23.131 --> 00:04:24.345
Exit code 137.

80
00:04:24.567 --> 00:04:30.718
When a signal kills a process, the exit code is 128 plus the signal number.

81
00:04:30.718 --> 00:04:32.440
137 is 128 plus nine.

82
00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:39.904
You'll meet it again when Docker or Kubernetes kills a container that used too much memory.

83
00:04:40.733 --> 00:04:42.858
The same pair stops containers.

84
00:04:42.858 --> 00:04:47.928
docker stop sends SIGTERM, waits ten seconds, and only then sends SIGKILL.

85
00:04:47.928 --> 00:04:51.697
Kubernetes does the same, with a thirty second default.

86
00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:55.130
The knock comes first, and the guard waits outside.

87
00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:58.804
So a program that handles SIGTERM gets to finish its work.

88
00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:00.894
Knock with SIGTERM first.

89
00:05:00.894 --> 00:05:04.615
Keep kill -9 for a process that ignores the knock.

90
00:05:05.433 --> 00:05:06.666
So, back to Friday.

91
00:05:06.666 --> 00:05:09.197
The script was missing one execute bit.

92
00:05:09.197 --> 00:05:13.350
The worker deserved a polite knock, and a few seconds to finish.

93
00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:16.075
Neither needed the master key or security.

94
00:05:16.075 --> 00:05:20.293
The moral: name the real cause before you reach for the shortcut.

95
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.103
A quick check.

96
00:05:22.103 --> 00:05:26.122
A file shows R W dash, R dash dash, dash dash dash.

97
00:05:26.122 --> 00:05:29.038
It belongs to user app and group ops.

98
00:05:29.038 --> 00:05:31.875
Dana is in ops, but isn't the owner.

99
00:05:31.875 --> 00:05:33.215
What can Dana do?

100
00:05:36.433 --> 00:05:37.329
Read it only.

101
00:05:37.329 --> 00:05:44.425
Dana is not the owner but is in the group, so the group's three slots decide, and they say R dash dash.
